\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Per-type effect sizes ($\Delta$pp, ambiguous minus unambiguous) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. All types produce significant increases. Types form a risk gradient---mid-range equivalence cluster, incompleteness as upper bound, coreferential as lower bound---partly reflecting an information-addition confound for incompleteness. The gradient structure is margin-dependent---see \S\ref{sec:results} for sensitivity analysis.}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Layer Effect (C3)}

Specification-layer types trend higher than linguistic-layer types (44.0\% vs.\ 37.9\%, $p = 0.017$), but C3 is inconclusive: the effect does not survive Holm--Bonferroni correction, controlling for token delta reverses the direction, and cross-layer type overlap (conditional precedence 36.8\% vs.\ scopal 39.4\%) further undermines the distinction. Type-level distinctions (C2) hold independently of layer grouping. We accordingly treat the two-layer structure as organizational convenience rather than a theoretical claim.

\subsection{Model $\times$ Type Interaction (C4) \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:c4}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and ambiguity type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Models judged by GPT-5.4: Claude, GPT-4.1, Qwen3; by GPT-4.1: GPT-5.4, DeepSeek. Authorization scope varies 52\,pp across models (upper bound; $\kappa = 0.385$), with within-judge-group ranges of 34\,pp (GPT-5.4-judged) vs.\ 4\,pp (GPT-4.1-judged). Incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%). (C4 interaction is authorization-scope-specific; excluding it yields $p = 0.97$.)}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20, 2976) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; $\mathrm{df} = (5{-}1) \times (6{-}1) = 20$; 2.9\% of variance; confirmed by logistic regression likelihood ratio test: $\chi^2(20) = 47.20$, $p = 5.5 \times 10^{-4}$) concentrates in specific type--model combinations.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three suggestive patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{most model-differentiating type} (52 pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), though this type has the lowest judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$; \S\ref{sec:limitations}), so absolute rates carry more uncertainty than for other types; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{consistent vulnerability} (46--62\%, 16 pp range); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant type} (34--40\%, 6 pp range). The authorization scope ranking (Claude $<$ Qwen3 $<$ GPT-4.1) is stable within the GPT-5.4 judge subset (bootstrap ranking preservation: 99.5\%, $B = 10{,}000$; pairwise orderings $\geq$99.5\%), controlling for judge identity. The 34\,pp within-judge range (GPT-5.4-judged subset) far exceeds measurement noise, but given the low cross-judge $\kappa$, we characterize this pattern as suggestive rather than definitive. Both judges belong to the OpenAI model family; cross-family evaluation (e.g., Claude as judge) would provide independent confirmation. Excluding authorization scope, the residual interaction vanishes ($p = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{app:c4_sans_auth}), confirming that the C4 interaction concentrates in this single type.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Differential nocuity.}
Building on \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}'s nocuous/innocuous distinction, our results are consistent with a \emph{differential nocuity} hypothesis \citep{willis_nocuous_2008}: risk depends on ambiguity type (base vulnerability), safety training (selective mitigation), and disambiguation channel availability. Types with explicit decision points (e.g., authorization scope) appear more amenable to both safety training and prompted intervention than gap-filling types (e.g., incompleteness), where mitigation attempts can backfire (see below).

\paragraph{Taxonomy as intervention design.}
Type membership explains only 0.4\% of violation variance versus 22.4\% for individual clause wording, because type differences manifest only under ambiguous conditions (all types produce $\sim$7\% violations when unambiguous). However, the taxonomy's value lies not in predicting violation rates but in predicting failure \emph{mechanisms}: each type maps to a mechanistically distinct failure pattern with cross-model consistency (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). Structural misparse dominates scopal and conditional precedence types (5/5 models each), information gap-filling dominates incompleteness and lexical types ($\geq$3/5), and boundary violation dominates authorization scope exclusively (5/5, with zero conservative refusals). This near-deterministic type$\to$mechanism mapping enables targeted intervention design---parse constraints for structural misparse, information supplementation for gap-filling, boundary enforcement for authorization scope---even though type membership is a poor predictor of individual clause risk.

\paragraph{Prompt-based mitigation.}
A matched-pair mitigation study on GPT-5.4 ($n = 50$ per type; Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) finds generic awareness prompting ineffective ($\Delta = +1.3$\,pp), with type-specific results mirroring the mechanism taxonomy: authorization scope improves ($-12$\,pp, $p = 0.070$) but incompleteness \emph{worsens} ($+14$\,pp, $p = 0.118$). Opposite directions under different prompt formulations (incompleteness: $-$3.4\,pp in v1 vs.\ $+$14\,pp in v2) reinforce policy-design-level solutions over prompt-level fixes.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
A proof-of-concept classifier (Appendix~\ref{app:detection}) achieves F1\,=\,0.81 for binary detection but only 0.34 for incompleteness---the highest-risk type is hardest to detect. With disambiguation neglect in $>$89\% of cases, type-aware auditing and externalized detection \citep{chantree_2006,ferrari_ambiguity_2019} are necessary for policy-governed deployments.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Scope.} We evaluate seven API-accessible models from six providers (Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); smaller open-source models (7B--70B) may exhibit different vulnerability patterns. $\tau^2$-bench covers retail and airline customer service only---effects in other domains remain untested, though within-study cross-domain consistency is encouraging. Our matched-pair design requires synthetic ambiguity construction, which may not capture the full distribution of naturally occurring ambiguities; realism validation (\S\ref{sec:method}) confirms lexical comparability but cannot rule out distributional gaps. Coreferential ambiguity was excluded from mitigation and replication experiments due to benchmark-specific structural constraints (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}).

\paragraph{Measurement.} Each model is evaluated by a single cross-judge, confounding judge identity with model identity; within-judge-group analyses confirm C1 individually (both $p < 0.001$) but not C3 (Appendix~\ref{app:judge_group_ranking}), and cross-model type ranking concordance is only moderate (Appendix~\ref{app:kendall_w}). Most critically, cross-judge-group type rankings show near-zero correlation ($\rho = 0.09$, $p = 0.87$; Appendix~\ref{app:judge_group_ranking}), meaning that the C2 gradient's mid-tier ordering does not generalize across judge groups---though excluding authorization scope yields strong concordance ($\rho = 0.90$, $p = 0.037$), and incompleteness as the highest-risk type is robust to this variation. Per-type reliability varies---authorization scope has the lowest agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$) and per-type human--LLM estimates rest on small cell sizes with wide confidence intervals (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement})---while failure mode coding achieves moderate agreement ($\kappa = 0.52$), with minority categories less reliable. Both judges belong to the OpenAI model family, so systematic leniency toward same-family outputs or severity toward competitors cannot be ruled out; cross-family judge evaluation (e.g., Claude judging GPT traces) would strengthen C4 conclusions. ICC is computed within the OpenAI model family (GPT-5.4 across runs); run-to-run variance for non-OpenAI models remains unmeasured.

\paragraph{Confounds.} Incompleteness resolution necessarily adds policy content, creating an information asymmetry; our logistic regression controls and coreferential lower-bound analysis mitigate but do not eliminate this confound (\S\ref{sec:results}). Our primary analysis uses a moderate-or-above severity threshold; switching to any-violation yields consistent results, with all four claims remaining significant and concordant type rankings.

\section*{Data and Code Availability}
The 300 clause pairs, evaluation pipeline, analysis scripts, and raw episode traces will be released upon publication at \url{https://github.com/anonymous/policy-ambiguity-safety}.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity: a property that has been studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} (i.e., harmful) ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared organizational context ensures convergent interpretation. In human RE workflows, most ambiguity is innocuous because developers share domain knowledge, can consult colleagues, and iteratively clarify requirements.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. We construct 300 matched clause pairs across a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy and inject them into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents. Five models interact with the modified policies across 2,997 episodes. A cross-judge evaluation pipeline classifies violation severity on a four-tier scale.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers---linguistic and specification, including specification-level gaps that produce equivalent behavioral effects in LLM agents (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results show that all policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents ($+34.0$ pp violation rate increase, OR $= 15.1$), with type primarily determining severity. Incompleteness is consistently high (46--62\% across all models); an exploratory model$\times$type interaction (\S\ref{sec:c4}) suggests differential vulnerability across type--model combinations. Agents exhibit disambiguation neglect in over 89\% of cases, with fewer than 11\% showing any disambiguation awareness (4.6\% overall; \S\ref{sec:analysis}).

Our contributions are: (1)~the first matched-pair causal study of policy ambiguity's safety impact with perturbation control (\S\ref{sec:method}), observed across five primary models and confirmed in two additional replication models; (2)~incompleteness as a consistent upper-bound risk type across all models and margins, with a variance decomposition showing that individual clause wording (22.4\%) dominates type membership (0.4\%) and model identity (1.0\%)---motivating clause-level auditing while the type taxonomy retains value for differential intervention design (\S\ref{sec:discussion}); and (3)~an error analysis quantifying \emph{Disambiguation Neglect} in consequential agentic settings---extending LLM ambiguity-collapse findings \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} to agentic settings---with $\leq$11\% per-model awareness (4.6\% overall), and \emph{systematic nocuity}: 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations, indicating clause-level risk factors (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

